 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S445-009\",\n \"clauses\": {\"solve_area_problem\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Ana has two rectangular flower beds: Bed 1 is {l1} {lu} by {w1} {lu} and Bed 2 is {l2} {lu} by {w2} {lu}. What is the combined total area of both flower beds in {unit_label}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 asks the learner to solve a two-part area problem: the combined total area of two rectangles. Its lu is printed only as m and its unit_label only as sq m, so the units agree. Its l1 and w1 are drawn from 2 to 10, and its l2 from 2, 3, 4 and 5 and its w2 from 2 to 6. These stay within Grade 3 multiplication.\"}, \"word_problem_context\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Ana has two rectangular flower beds: Bed 1 is {l1} {lu} by {w1} {lu} and Bed 2 is {l2} {lu} by {w2} {lu}. What is the combined total area of both flower beds in {unit_label}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 is a named-character word problem in the sentence and question_text fields, with a concrete context (flower beds) that a Grade 3 Q1 learner can read. Its values stay in the declared domains.\"}, \"extract_area_relationship_from_context\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"A rectangular {item_noun} has an area of {rect_area} {unit_label} and a {known_name} of {known_val} {lu}. What is its {other_name} in {lu}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 gives an area and one side in a context and asks the learner to extract the relationship between area and the other side. Its lu is basis-linked to unit_label (the length units of the two unit_labels), so the units agree. The keyed-answer divisibility is not stated by a domain; the F11 hint's observed quotients are all integers, which is consistent with the generator enforcing it.\"}, \"area_attribute\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"A square {item_noun} measures {s} {lu} on each side. What is its area in {unit_label}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 asks for the area of a figure, which is the area attribute. Its lu is printed only as m and its unit_label only as sq m, so the units agree.\"}, \"square_figure\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"A square {item_noun} measures {s} {lu} on each side. What is its area in {unit_label}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 is a square: it gives one side length and asks for the area. Its s is drawn from 2 to 5, which is within Grade 3 scope.\"}, \"rectangle_figure\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F02\", \"template\": \"A rectangular {item_noun} has an area of {rect_area} {unit_label} and a {known_name} of {known_val} {lu}. What is its {other_name} in {lu}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F02 is a rectangle, with a length or width and an area. Its units are linked by basis, so the length unit matches the area unit.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F04\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"A rectangular {item_noun} is {l} {lu} long and {w} {lu} wide. What is the area of the {item_noun} in {unit_label}?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F04 is the failing family. Its lu slot draws from the length-unit list cm and m, and its unit_label slot draws from the area-unit list sq cm and sq m. Its unit_label is derived from the key unit and its lu from the key length_unit, and the packet gives no basis linking them, unlike F02 (where lu is basis-linked to the unit_labels) and F05 and F06 (where only one unit pair is printed). So the declared domains allow a rectangle given in m to be asked for its area in sq cm, or a rectangle given in cm to be asked for its area in sq m. A Grade 3 Q1 learner would need a unit conversion to answer that, which is beyond the lc's scope. Other families were checked and not failed on this packet's evidence. F14 and F22 print only positive differences, consistent with the generator enforcing Plot B larger. F02 and F11 print integer quotients only. F12 has a note: its members for area and cost (10, 12, 3, 4) do not account for